<header><p class="enc-kicker">Religion / idea · deity</p><h1 class="enc-title">Astarte</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Ashtart</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Astarte was a major goddess of the ancient <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/levant">Levant</a>, worshipped by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/phoenicians">Phoenicians</a>, Canaanites and others as a deity of love, fertility and war. Known in Phoenician as Ashtart and related to the Mesopotamian Ishtar, she had great temples at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sidon">Sidon</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tyre">Tyre</a> and Kition in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cyprus">Cyprus</a>, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greeks">Greeks</a> identified her with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aphrodite">Aphrodite</a>. Her cult spread with Phoenician colonies across the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mediterranean">Mediterranean</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/astarte.jpg" alt="Astarte" width="320" height="480" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Ismoon 21:17, 4 January 2022 (UTC) · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Astarte_-_7th_cent_AD_-_Museo_Arqueol%C3%B3gico_de_Sevilla-2.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>Astarte is the Hellenized form of the Ancient Near Eastern goddess ʿAṯtart, the Northwest Semitic counterpart of the East Semitic goddess Ishtar. Her name is first recorded at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ebla">Ebla</a> in the 3rd millennium BCE, in the forms Aštarta and Ištarta. Her main cult centre was <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mari">Mari</a>, and she was originally associated with Amorite cities such as Ugarit, Emar, Mari and Ebla. The meaning of her name remains unclear. She was worshipped from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bronze-age">Bronze Age</a> through classical antiquity.</p><p>At Ugarit ʿAṯtartu had no astral aspect and had only a small part in myth, but she is often named in ritual and administrative texts, which suggests importance for the institution of kingship. She was regularly paired with the goddess Anat, with whom she shared beauty and a love of war, and the two are described hunting together. In the Baʿal Epic she is an ally of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baal">Baal</a> against the sea god Yam.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">What it is</h2><p>Across cultures Astarte was linked with war, sexuality, royal power, beauty and healing, and at Ugarit and Emar with hunting. Her symbol was the lion, and she was associated with the horse and so with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chariot">chariots</a>, and perhaps the dove. The only images identified with certainty as Astarte show her as a combatant on horseback or in a chariot. In New Kingdom Egypt she was known as a West Semitic war goddess, with a cult attested from the reign of Amenhotep II in the 15th century BCE, and was sometimes paired with the god <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/resheph">Resheph</a>.</p><p>In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iron-age">Iron Age</a> the Canaanites worshipped her as ʿAštart. In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/phoenicia">Phoenicia</a> in the 1st millennium BCE she overshadowed the other Semitic goddesses and became the main personification of a less warlike and more sensual vitality. As a fertility goddess she promoted love and presided over the reproduction of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cattle">cattle</a> and family growth, and was the consort of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hadad">Hadad</a> or <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baal">Baal</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reception and legacy</h2><p>Her cult spread to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cyprus">Cyprus</a>, where she may have merged with an ancient Cypriot goddess who may in turn have been adopted into the Greek pantheon to form <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aphrodite">Aphrodite</a>. Phoenicians introduced her cult in their colonies on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iberia">Iberian Peninsula</a>. At Memphis a temple of the goddess stood within the temple of Ptah, and from at least the 6th century BCE she was identified with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/isis">Isis</a>. Lucian of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/samosata">Samosata</a> wrote that the temple of Astarte in the territory of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sidon">Sidon</a> was sacred to Europa, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/plutarch">Plutarch</a> names her as the queen of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/byblos">Byblos</a> in the story of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/osiris">Osiris</a>.</p><p>The Masoretic vocalisation of her name in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hebrew">Hebrew</a> text, ʿAštōret, is thought by most scholars to put the vowels of the Hebrew word for shame on her consonants. In later kabbalistic and occult texts the name Astaroth was given to a male demon bearing little resemblance to the ancient goddess.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Astarte” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>…II, discovered in 1855, calls him 'king of the Sidonians' and says his mother was a priestess of <b>Ashtart</b>, 'the goddess of the Sidonians', naming Eshmun and Baal Sidon, 'Lord of Sidon', as the chief gods and giving Ashtart the title Ashtart-Shem-Baal, 'Ashtart the name of the Lord',…</blockquote><a href="/empire/sidon">Sidon · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>…were shaped by the older peoples of the Aegean and the civilisations of the Near East: Hesiod’s myth of the succession of the gods resembles Hurrian and Hittite myths of the kingship in heaven, and Aphrodite has much in common with the Near Eastern goddess Ishtar and <b>Astarte</b>.</blockquote><a href="/religion/ancient-greek-religion">Ancient Greek religion · Origins</a></li><li><blockquote>…Baal Hadad, lord of storms, thunder and lightning and king of the gods; Anat, virgin goddess of war; <b>Astarte</b>, goddess of war, hunting and love; Dagon, god of grain and father of Baal; Resheph, god of plague and of healing; Yam, god of the sea and rivers; the…</blockquote><a href="/religion/canaanite-religion">Canaanite religion · Beliefs &amp; Practice</a></li><li><blockquote>At Pyrgi, the port of Caere, gold tablets of about 500 BCE record in Etruscan and Phoenician a dedication by Thefarie Velianas, ruler of Caere, to Uni, called <b>Astarte</b> in the Phoenician text.</blockquote><a href="/culture/etruscans">Etruscans · Beliefs &amp; Worldview</a></li><li><blockquote>At Sidon the royal family also served the goddess <b>Astarte</b> as priests, as the inscribed sarcophagi of King Tabnit and his son Eshmunazar II record.</blockquote><a href="/culture/phoenicians">Phoenicians · Society</a></li><li><blockquote>It is assumed that, like other Mediterranean peoples, the Tartessians were polytheists, and it is believed that through Phoenician influence they worshipped the goddess <b>Astarte</b> or Potnia and the male god Baal or Melkart.</blockquote><a href="/culture/tartessos">Tartessos · Beliefs &amp; Worldview</a></li></ul></section>
